# Content Security Policy + Angular: Inline Styles & Trusted Types

## 1. What is it?

Content Security Policy (CSP) is an HTTP response header (or an equivalent `<meta http-equiv="Content-Security-Policy">` tag) that tells the browser which sources of scripts, styles, images, fonts, connections, and other resources are allowed to load or execute on a page. It's an allowlist enforced by the browser itself, not by your application code — if a script or style doesn't match an allowed source, the browser simply refuses to run or apply it, and fires a `securitypolicyviolation` event you can observe.

Trusted Types is a separate, narrower CSP-adjacent mechanism (`require-trusted-types-for 'script'` plus a `trusted-types <policy-names>` directive) that locks down DOM XSS sinks specifically — `innerHTML`, `outerHTML`, `document.write`, `<script>` src assignment, and similar — so that only values produced by an explicitly created, allow-listed "policy" object can be assigned to them. A plain string is rejected even if script-src would otherwise allow it.

## 2. Why introduced?

CSP exists to blunt the impact of markup/script injection (XSS): even if an attacker manages to get their payload into the page's HTML (via an unescaped user input, a compromised third-party script, a stored XSS bug, etc.), a strict CSP means the browser won't execute or render it unless it comes from an explicitly trusted origin. `style-src` closes off a lesser-known but real sub-case: injected `<style>` tags can be used for data exfiltration (CSS-based attribute/history sniffing) or UI redress attacks, so strict deployments block inline styles too, not just inline scripts.

Trusted Types was introduced because `style-src`/`script-src` alone don't stop *DOM-based* XSS: an attacker doesn't need to inject a `<script>` tag if they can get your own already-trusted code to call `element.innerHTML = attackerControlledString`. Trusted Types forces every sink write to go through a reviewed policy function, so a raw, unreviewed string can no longer reach the DOM even via your own code.

## 3. How does it work?

The browser parses the CSP (header or meta tag) into directives — `default-src`, `style-src`, `script-src`, `require-trusted-types-for`, `trusted-types`, etc. — each naming allowed sources (`'self'`, specific origins, `'unsafe-inline'`, `'unsafe-eval'`, nonces, hashes). Before rendering a style, executing a script, or writing to a Trusted-Types-guarded sink, the browser checks the action against the matching directive; if there's no match, the action is silently blocked and a `securitypolicyviolation` event fires on `document` with details (`violatedDirective`, `blockedURI`, `effectiveDirective`).

Angular interacts with this in two independent ways:
- **Component styles.** Angular's default view encapsulation (Emulated) works by injecting a real `<style>` element into the document at runtime for every component that declares a `styles: []` array, with a CSS attribute selector Angular adds for scoping. That injected tag has no nonce and no hash, so to the browser/CSP it is indistinguishable from an attacker-injected inline `<style>` tag.
- **Trusted Types.** Angular's `DomSanitizer` registers its own internal Trusted Types policy (commonly named `'angular'`), so bindings like `[innerHTML]` and `[src]` continue to work under a Trusted-Types-enforcing CSP — the sanitizer produces real `TrustedHTML`/`TrustedScriptURL` values through that policy. Any *raw*, native DOM write that bypasses Angular (e.g. `elementRef.nativeElement.innerHTML = someString`) is not covered, and gets blocked by Trusted Types like any other unreviewed sink write.

